1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:05,060
Why did almost 33,000 Allied soldiers surrender here after just one day of fighting?

2
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:10,120
A year earlier, the same fortress had held out against Rommel for almost eight months.

3
00:00:10,420 --> 00:00:16,700
June 1942: Rommel's Afrika Korps against South Africans, Britons and Indians.

4
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:20,440
At Gazala, Rommel had outflanked the British line in the south.

5
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:25,560
Of around 850 British tanks, only about 70 were still fit for action.

6
00:00:25,580 --> 00:00:27,860
Churchill still ordered Tobruk to be held.

7
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:37,140
But the 50-kilometre defensive ring had fallen into disrepair, and the new commander, General Klopper, had led his division for barely a month.

8
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:42,120
On 20 June at 5:20 a.m., Stukas bombed one corner of the ring.

9
00:00:42,220 --> 00:00:47,740
Engineers bridged the anti-tank ditch, and by 7:45 the German tanks were through.

10
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:50,640
By 1 p.m. they held the heights above the town.

11
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:52,540
By evening, the harbour was burning.

12
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:54,520
The next morning, Klopper surrendered.

13
00:00:54,540 --> 00:00:58,860
For South Africa, it was the worst military defeat in its history.

14
00:00:59,020 --> 00:01:01,720
Hitler promoted Rommel to field marshal.

15
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:07,300
With the captured fuel, Rommel drove on into Egypt until he was stopped at El Alamein.

16
00:01:07,340 --> 00:01:13,960
Today, more than 6,000 German dead lie in a memorial near Tobruk that itself looks like a fortress.
